when thinking about the life of bruce  wayne many can agree that his most  famous life event is his most tragic  most iterations of the batman story at  some point have depicted the event that  sealed his fate the murder of his  parents it's a moment that changes the  course of his life forever driving him  to create a symbol that the criminals of  gotham will fear matt reeves the batman  unlike so many previous cinematic  incarnations of the character does not  tread on this familiar territory when  the batman picks up it is two years  after bruce has started wearing the cowl  he feels the trauma of his past deeply  but it is represented more in what is  not said than what is  i don't care what happens to me  bruce has become reclusive hiding away  in wayne tower with alfred played by  andy serkis only venturing out around  people when it will help him solve a  mystery there are many aspects about  this version of batman that are  heartbreaking bruce's self-imposed  isolation and his inability to connect  to other people in a meaningful way or  just a couple and in the midst of all  the murder and mayhem the film depicts  it is one scene in particular that shows  just how sad the life of gotham's dark  knight truly is throughout the film  bruce has made it abundantly clear how  alone he is he goes out at night invites  as many criminals as he can when he  comes home the only person who tries to  forge a connection with him is alfred  but it's a one-sided effort if this  continues it won't be long before you've  nothing left alfred is concerned about  bruce's nighttime activities and why a  serial killer is leaving him messages  but for all of alfred's attempts to take  care of bruce the young wayne pushes him  away he tells alfred that he isn't his  father and doesn't accept his guidance  this dynamic reaches its devastating  peak when the riddler sends a package to  wayne tower while it's meant for bruce  alfred opens it just as a bomb inside is  detonated alfred survives the attack but  he is terribly wounded when he regains  consciousness in the hospital he and  bruce have a loaded conversation full of  pain and unresolved trauma both alfred  and bruce have burdens they need to  unload upon one another and when they do  it finally brings them together when  alfred first wakes up from his injuries  bruce is there by his side but rather  than express sympathy the dark knight's  first response is to accuse his longtime  butler of keeping family secrets from  him bruce lashes out upset that he never  knew his father had a business  relationship with known mobster carmine  falcone however once the two resolved  this issue bruce admits to being afraid  for the first time since the death of  his parents to cope with the loss he  became a symbol devoid of any fear by  becoming the personification of  vengeance he ensured he would never feel  powerless again but bruce acknowledges  that when alfred's life was in danger  being batman did not help he admits that  alfred is one of the only people that  means something to him anymore and that  losing him would be just as bad as  losing his parents bruce wasn't the only  one who has been holding on to past pain  alfred holds immense guilt over the  deaths of the wanes as he tells his  young charge bruce was just a boy when  they died and alfred thinks he should  have done better to protect the wanes  not only that but he never felt equipped  to take care of a young bruce or raise  him properly after being thrust into a  parental role alfred remarks that bruce  needed a father and he never felt that  he was good enough to fill that role he  blames himself for bruce's emotional  withdrawal repression and rejection of  the wayne legacy that said it is clear  that alfred holds no ill-will towards  bruce despite how cold their  relationship appears earlier in the film  this scene marks the first time both men  have truly opened up to one another and  it allows them to finally begin healing  